    <item>
      <title>Cod and Eel and the Geopolitical Recipes Behind Bacalao and Unagi</title>
      <link>https://www.bloomberg.com/opinion/articles/2026-10-16/cod-and-eel-and-the-geopolitical-recipes-behind-bacalao-and-unagi</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">55b5bc14951efdc517f79558</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 03:00:07 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>Spain loves bacalao — a salted cod dish that it’s shared with the world during a centuries-long enthusiasm. So I was startled to hear an apology for eating too much of the fish from a Spaniard at a culinary conference in San Sebastian. It was an aside at a talk about the natural and commercial history of gadus morhua, but it reflected guilt from a catastrophic event more than 30 years ago: Canada’s 1992 moratorium on taking Atlantic cod from its territorial waters off Labrador and Newfoundland.</description>
      <dc:creator>Howard Chua-Eoan</dc:creator>
